{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773441208,"nonce":"klEGpiqMEU6SNIsHv4Qx9+LWbSo=","title":"auth-queue: daemon restart mid-execution leaves request stuck in \"executing\" forever","message":"Race condition: when a merge triggers a daemon restart (because dist/ changed), the executor process is killed before it can write the result back to the DB. The request stays stuck in status=executing with output=null indefinitely.\n\nRecovery logic in recoverStuckRequests():\n- Case 1 (output contains \"Merge made by\") does not apply — output is null\n- Case 2 (empty output, older than 5 min) eventually fires, but incorrectly marks it exit_code=1/failed, even if the merge actually succeeded\n\nObserved: b38dc46d (feature/push-fixes) — merge confirmed on main as 5cdb40b, but request stayed \"executing\". Workaround: DELETE via API.\n\nFix ideas:\n1. Before Case 2 marks a request as failed, check whether the head_branch was actually merged into base_branch (git merge-base --is-ancestor, or check if head commit appears in base log). If merged, mark completed (exit_code=0) rather than failed.\n2. Or: have auth-merge.sh write the result to the DB *before* triggering the daemon restart (currently it triggers launchctl restart synchronously, which kills the process before the API call).\n3. Or: write result to a recovery file on disk, and on startup read any unprocessed recovery files before the 5-min expiry check.\n\nOption 2 is probably the simplest — just reorder the post-merge steps so the DB write happens before the restart signal.","files":null}]}